1
00:00:11,710 --> 00:00:13,970
Hey there, it's me.

2
00:00:14,790 --> 00:00:17,870
Another day, another useful lesson from PREP.

3
00:00:18,470 --> 00:00:21,670
Welcome back to our IELTS writing course.

4
00:00:22,110 --> 00:00:27,010
To get a higher score in IELTS, you must use complex sentences.

5
00:00:27,930 --> 00:00:32,870
A complex sentence combines a main idea with extra information.

6
00:00:33,750 --> 00:00:36,630
Today, we will study the rules for three

7
00:00:36,630 --> 00:00:43,450
types of grammar – relative clauses, contracted relative clauses, and conditionals.

8
00:00:47,840 --> 00:00:51,400
First, let's look at the common structure of relative clauses.

9
00:00:52,300 --> 00:00:56,060
Subject, verb, comma, who, which, or that.

10
00:00:56,540 --> 00:01:00,540
Relative clauses start with words like who, which, or that.

11
00:01:00,980 --> 00:01:02,340
Who describes a person?

12
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:04,120
Which describes an object?

13
00:01:04,120 --> 00:01:10,180
However, which can also describe the entire idea before a comma.

14
00:01:10,900 --> 00:01:13,600
That is used for both people and things,

15
00:01:13,880 --> 00:01:17,780
but remember not to use that after a comma.

16
00:01:18,420 --> 00:01:22,440
In IELTS Writing Task 1, relative clauses are

17
00:01:22,440 --> 00:01:24,900
very useful in comparing data.

18
00:01:25,280 --> 00:01:28,380
You can describe any information in the graph

19
00:01:28,380 --> 00:01:31,380
by stating a date, and then compare it

20
00:01:31,380 --> 00:01:34,360
to another data by adding a which clause.

21
00:01:34,880 --> 00:01:36,460
Let's look at some examples.

22
00:01:37,540 --> 00:01:45,400
According to the bar chart, by far the highest percentage is subject interest, at 40%, 

23
00:01:45,400 --> 00:01:50,160
which is only 2% higher than that of gaining qualifications.

24
00:01:51,160 --> 00:01:57,840
Relative clauses can also be used to provide more information about a noun or a phrase.

25
00:01:58,040 --> 00:02:03,200
For example, online applications allowed people who

26
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:06,040
could not go to their offices to work from home.

27
00:02:06,620 --> 00:02:10,460
In this sentence, the relative clause explains more

28
00:02:10,460 --> 00:02:12,720
about a specific group of people.

29
00:02:13,160 --> 00:02:16,640
Without this clause, we wouldn't know why

30
00:02:16,640 --> 00:02:19,720
these specific people were working remotely.

31
00:02:20,380 --> 00:02:24,300
And finally, relative clauses are also used to

32
00:02:24,300 --> 00:02:27,700
connect and show the relation between two phrases.

33
00:02:36,100 --> 00:02:42,120
The word "which" refers to the entire situation mentioned before the comma, 

34
00:02:42,340 --> 00:02:53,660
using this structure connects the cause, bad communication, to the result, low productivity.

35
00:02:56,590 --> 00:03:00,230
When a relative pronoun is a subject and

36
00:03:00,230 --> 00:03:03,410
the verb is passive, we can contract the clause.

37
00:03:03,910 --> 00:03:07,650
Let's look at this sentence in Sample Band 6 essay.

38
00:03:08,550 --> 00:03:11,970
40% of people in the survey think

39
00:03:11,970 --> 00:03:15,430
individuals should pay the costs of each course,

40
00:03:15,950 --> 00:03:19,170
followed by employers at 35%.

41
00:03:19,450 --> 00:03:24,430
In this sentence, followed by is a contracted relative clause.

42
00:03:24,430 --> 00:03:27,710
The full version is, which is followed by.

43
00:03:28,650 --> 00:03:31,570
When the relative pronoun is the subject of

44
00:03:31,570 --> 00:03:34,410
the relative clause, and the verb is in

45
00:03:34,410 --> 00:03:38,050
the passive form, the relative clause is contracted

46
00:03:38,050 --> 00:03:40,610
to the past participle of the verb.

47
00:03:41,450 --> 00:03:44,170
We have this structure that you can apply

48
00:03:45,920 --> 00:03:48,780
easily in writing Task 1 essays,

49
00:03:48,780 --> 00:03:52,420
subject, verb, followed by a noun or noun phrase.

50
00:03:52,730 --> 00:03:56,330
However, please note that followed by is used

51
00:03:56,330 --> 00:03:58,830
to say what comes after something else 

52
00:03:58,830 --> 00:04:01,490
in a list or ordered set of things.

53
00:04:07,120 --> 00:04:09,510
Similarly, we have another common contracted

54
00:04:09,510 --> 00:04:15,570
relative clause, subject, verb, compared with a noun or noun phrase.

55
00:04:16,070 --> 00:04:21,350
In this structure, compared to with is also a contracted relative clause.

56
00:04:21,470 --> 00:04:25,450
The full version is, which is compared to with.

57
00:04:25,930 --> 00:04:29,710
This structure can be used to compare any two subjects.

58
00:04:30,270 --> 00:04:56,990
This sentence can be rewritten as each course, compared with employers at 35%.

59
00:05:01,730 --> 00:05:07,230
Conditionals are used to show a logical link between a condition and its result.

60
00:05:08,110 --> 00:05:11,670
This is essential for describing processes or making arguments.

61
00:05:12,290 --> 00:05:18,550
The structure is if or unless subject, verb, comma, subject, verb.

62
00:05:18,770 --> 00:05:22,030
Please note that unless means if not.

63
00:05:22,490 --> 00:05:25,510
In both cases, if you start the sentence

64
00:05:25,510 --> 00:05:30,250
with the conjunction if or unless, you must

65
00:05:30,250 --> 00:05:34,270
use a comma to separate the condition from the result.

66
00:05:35,210 --> 00:05:36,630
Here are some examples.

67
00:05:37,550 --> 00:05:41,790
If the jam is too thin, pectin is added to thicken the mixture.

68
00:05:42,450 --> 00:05:45,450
This sentence is from a Writing Task 1

69
00:05:45,450 --> 00:05:49,310
essay describing the process of making cherry jam.

70
00:05:49,990 --> 00:06:04,610
Another example of conditional structure is This

71
00:06:04,610 --> 00:06:09,090
sentence is the same as If authorities do

72
00:06:09,090 --> 00:06:14,710
not implement stricter environmental regulations, large corporations will

73
00:06:14,710 --> 00:06:18,070
continue to prioritise profit over sustainability.

74
00:06:19,070 --> 00:06:20,310
Great job everyone!

75
00:06:20,950 --> 00:06:25,590
Identifying these structures is the first step to using them.

76
00:06:26,270 --> 00:06:29,790
Practice finding these in high-level model answers

77
00:06:29,790 --> 00:06:36,290
and then try to build them into your own Task 1 and Task 2 responses.

78
00:06:36,890 --> 00:06:42,230
Now let's have a quick review of what we learned today through some questions.

79
00:06:47,740 --> 00:06:51,060
Today's lesson covered a lot, so make sure

80
00:06:51,060 --> 00:06:55,860
to organise and review your notes to remember and apply the knowledge effectively.

81
00:06:56,580 --> 00:06:57,780
You've all done well.

82
00:06:58,460 --> 00:07:02,740
Now take some time to relax and get ready for the next lessons.

83
00:07:02,740 --> 00:07:07,180
I am Anna, goodbye and see you next time.

